.TH SHMGET 2
.SH NAME
shmget, shmat, shmdt, shmctl \- System V shared memory operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.B #include <sys/ipc.h>
.B #include <sys/shm.h>
.PP
.B int shmget(key_t key, size_t size, int shmflg);
.B void *shmat(int shmid, const void *addr, int shmflg);
.B int shmdt(const void *addr);
.B int shmctl(int shmid, int cmd, struct shmid_ds *buf);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Shmget
finds or creates a shared memory segment.
.B IPC_PRIVATE
always creates a new segment.
For other keys,
.B IPC_CREAT
creates an absent segment and
.B IPC_EXCL
rejects an existing one.
The low nine bits of
.I shmflg
set creation permissions.
The initial implementation supports 32 live segments, each from one byte
through 64 MiB.
.PP
.I Shmat
maps the complete segment into the calling process.
A null
.I addr
selects free VM space.
A non-null address must be page aligned unless
.B SHM_RND
is specified.
.B SHM_RDONLY
creates a read-only attachment; other attachments require read and write
permission.
At most eight System V attachments and 32 total VM map entries are available
per process.
.PP
Attachments are inherited across
.IR fork (2).
They are detached by
.IR shmdt ,
successful
.IR execve (2),
or process exit.
The address passed to
.I shmdt
must be the value returned by
.IR shmat .
Partial
.IR munmap (2)
and replacement with
.B MAP_FIXED
are rejected for System V attachments so attachment accounting remains
atomic.
.PP
.I Shmctl
supports
.BR IPC_STAT ,
.BR IPC_SET ,
and
.BR IPC_RMID .
.B IPC_RMID
removes the key and identifier immediately, but existing attachments,
including those inherited by a later fork, remain valid until the final
detach.
The key may meanwhile name a new independent segment.
Sequence bits in identifiers prevent a stale identifier from selecting a
reused table slot.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.I Shmget
returns a non-negative identifier and
.I shmat
returns the mapping address on success.
The other calls return zero on success.
On failure the integer calls return -1 and
.I shmat
returns
.BR (void *)-1 .
.SH ERRORS
Errors include
.BR EACCES
for denied access,
.BR EEXIST
for an exclusive create of an existing key,
.BR EINVAL
for invalid flags, sizes, addresses, commands, or stale identifiers,
.BR ENOENT
for an absent key without
.BR IPC_CREAT ,
.BR ENOMEM
for unavailable address space, and
.BR ENOSPC
when a fixed table or VM resource is exhausted.
.SH SEE ALSO
execve(2), fork(2), mmap(2), shm_open(2), sysctl(8)
